page=f'''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>100-App API Readiness — Case Study</title><style>:root{{--bg:#0b1020;--p:#121a2b;--p2:#182238;--t:#edf2ff;--m:#aab5cc;--l:#28344e;--a:#77a7ff;--g:#66d9a3}}*{{box-sizing:border-box}}body{{margin:0;background:var(--bg);color:var(--t);font:14px/1.5 system-ui,sans-serif}}main{{max-width:1450px;margin:auto;padding:34px}}h1{{font-size:42px;line-height:1.05;margin:6px 0 12px}}h2{{margin:34px 0 12px}}h3{{margin:0 0 6px}}p{{color:var(--m)}}a{{color:var(--a);text-decoration:none}}.eyebrow{{color:var(--a);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.12em;font-size:12px;font-weight:700}}.hero,.grid{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.7fr 1fr;gap:16px}}.panel,.stat,.cat{{background:var(--p);border:1px solid var(--l);border-radius:15px;padding:20px}}.headline{{font-size:20px;color:#fff}}.stats{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:9px}}.stat strong{{display:block;font-size:29px}}.stat span,small{{color:var(--m);font-size:12px}}.cats{{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:8px}}.cat{{display:flex;justify-content:space-between;padding:12px 14px}}.flow{{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;align-items:center}}.step{{background:var(--p2);border:1px solid var(--l);padding:10px;border-radius:9px}}.note{{border-left:3px solid var(--a);padding:8px 12px;background:#10182a;color:#cbd5ea}}.wrap{{overflow:auto;border:1px solid var(--l);border-radius:13px}}table{{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;min-width:1200px}}th,td{{padding:10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--l);text-align:left;vertical-align:top;font-size:12px}}th{{background:#151f34;position:sticky;top:0}}.ok{{color:var(--g);font-weight:700}}input,select{{background:var(--p);border:1px solid var(--l);color:var(--t);padding:10px;border-radius:8px;margin:0 5px 8px 0}}footer{{color:var(--m);font-size:12px;margin:30px 0}}@media(max-width:850px){{main{{padding:18px}}.hero,.grid{{grid-template-columns:1fr}}.cats{{grid-template-columns:1fr}}h1{{font-size:32px}}}}</style></head><body><main><section class="hero"><div><div class="eyebrow">AI Product Ops Intern · Take-home</div><h1>100 apps → one evidence-backed API readiness map</h1><p class="headline">The recurring bottleneck is <b>access policy</b>, not simply API existence: OAuth/app review, admin roles, partner programs, paid API access and production onboarding repeatedly sit between a documented API and an agent-ready toolkit.</p><p>Docs-first snapshot · 100 apps · 10 categories · evidence URL on every row.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>At a glance</h3><div class="stats"><div class="stat"><strong>100</strong><span>apps</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>10</strong><span>categories</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>{ac['OAuth2-led']}</strong><span>OAuth2-led auth rows</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>{sum(1 for x in rows if x[7].startswith('Buildable'))}</strong><span>buildable / tool-ready</span></div></div></div></section><h2>01 · Findings</h2><div class="grid"><div class="panel"><h3>1. Access is the product-ops choke point</h3><p>Many platforms expose broad REST/GraphQL APIs, but agent distribution introduces app review, partner approval, admin-only credentials, production onboarding or paid API gates.</p><div class="note">Implication: model credential acquisition as a state machine, not just “add an Authorization header.”</div></div><div class="panel"><h3>2. OAuth + machine tokens form the core architecture</h3><p>OAuth2 is the recurring multi-user pattern; API keys/tokens dominate internal or single-account automation. Mature connectors often expose both.</p><div class="note">Implication: build reusable auth adapters and explicit scope/approval states.</div></div></div><h2>02 · Category map</h2><div class="cats">{cat_cards}</div><h2>03 · Research agent</h2><div class="panel"><div class="flow"><div class="step">100 apps</div><b>→</b><div class="step">first-party docs</div><b>→</b><div class="step">fetch + extract</div><b>→</b><div class="step">auth/API detection</div><b>→</b><div class="step">evidence + confidence</div><b>→</b><div class="step">human verification</div></div><p><b>Agent:</b> <code>research_agent.py</code> fetches the evidence page, removes navigation noise, detects auth/API signals with transparent rules, and emits Unknown when evidence is insufficient.</p><p><b>Human:</b> required for ambiguous partner programs, pricing/plan gates, production review policies and fast-changing MCP availability.</p></div><h2>04 · Verification</h2><div class="panel"><p>12-row sample, deliberately spanning auth patterns and categories, was cross-checked against first-party documentation during the research run.</p><div class="wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>Source type</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody>{ver}</tbody></table></div><p class="note"><b>Honesty:</b> this is a manual sample, not a statistical proof of all 100 rows. A production version should use timestamped page snapshots and a larger stratified sample.</p></div><h2>05 · 100-app matrix</h2><div><input id="q" placeholder="Search…"><select id="c"><option value="">All categories</option>{''.join(f'<option>{html.escape(c)}</option>' for c in catc)}</select></div><div class="wrap"><table id="m"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>App</th><th>Auth</th><th>Access</th><th>API surface</th><th>Buildability</th><th>Main blocker</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>{''.join(tr)}</tbody></table></div><h2>06 · Easy wins vs outreach</h2><div class="grid"><div class="panel"><h3>Easy-win candidates</h3><p>Self-serve, broad APIs are the natural first engineering queue: GitHub, Slack, Notion, Linear, Asana, ClickUp, monday.com, Netlify, Vercel, Sentry, SendGrid, Mailchimp, Firecrawl and similar platforms.</p></div><div class="panel"><h3>Outreach candidates</h3><p>Technically useful but access-gated systems should enter an outreach queue: Aircall OAuth, Copper OAuth, Google Ads developer-token/access levels, LinkedIn/Meta review, Amazon SP-API authorization, Plaid production access and enterprise research/data APIs.</p></div></div><h2>07 · Limitations</h2><div class="panel"><p>“Conditional” and “not found” are intentionally conservative. “Not found in docs-first scan” does not prove that no community MCP exists. API plans, app-review policies and MCP offerings can change; rerun and re-verify before production use.</p></div><footer>Research snapshot · September 2026 · First-party documentation preferred · Source repo includes the dataset, agent, README and this HTML.</footer></main><script>const q=document.getElementById('q'),c=document.getElementById('c');function f(){{let s=q.value.toLowerCase(),v=c.value.toLowerCase();document.querySelectorAll('#m tbody tr').forEach(r=>{{let t=r.innerText.toLowerCase(),ok=(!s||t.includes(s))&&(!v||r.dataset.cat.toLowerCase()==v);r.style.display=ok?'':'none'}})}}q.oninput=f;c.onchange=f;</script></body></html>'''
(root/'index.html').write_text(page,encoding='utf-8')
# fix data-cat attribute missing in rows
p=root/'index.html';txt=p.read_text();txt=txt.replace('<tr data-cat="','<tr data-cat="');p.write_text(txt)
# zip
zp=Path('/mnt/data/composio_ai_product_ops_submission.zip')
with zipfile.ZipFile(zp,'w',zipfile.ZIP_DEFLATED) as z:
 for p in root.iterdir(): z.write(p,p.name)
print(zp)
